  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1000 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday:\n\nFor this weekend into early next week, a longwave upper ridge \nholds its ground over the western Great Lakes, allowing \npredominantly dry and quiet weather to prevail. Surface high \npressure weakens but remains present across the Great lakes as \nwell, with a light east to southeast breeze expected, nearly \ncalm at times. Temperatures remain slightly below seasonal \nnorms. Portions of southwestern WI may observe some slight \nchances for rain in the forecast at times (mainly below 25% \nchance, confined south and west of MSN) as shortwave troughs \nattempt to crest the ridge. \n\nMonday night into Tuesday, an organized upper trough becomes\nestablished over the Dakotas / Nebraska, which will then track\neastward across the Midwest and likely result in strong\ncyclogenesis midweek. Rain chances spread into the region from\nwest to east on Tuesday, with rain looking likely at some point\nTuesday night or Wednesday as surface low pressure deepens and\ntracks northeastward past the region (exact track is uncertain). \n\nSheppard",
